WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.053
SignalR is a framework for real-time
communication.

2
00:00:03.133 --> 00:00:07.218
It helps an ASP.NET Core application keep
connected clients updated without forcing

3
00:00:07.298 --> 00:00:10.022
every interaction through a fresh request
and response.

4
00:00:10.102 --> 00:00:14.130
That convenience is powerful, but it does
not turn SignalR into a security boundary.

5
00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:16.990
The same application still has to decide
who is connected,

6
00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:20.191
what that connection may do, what data may
cross the hub boundary,

7
00:00:20.271 --> 00:00:22.086
and how the system behaves under load.

8
00:00:22.166 --> 00:00:25.230
A hub is both a server entry point and a
message delivery surface.

9
00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:27.523
Connected clients can invoke server
methods,

10
00:00:27.603 --> 00:00:31.461
and the server can send messages back to
one client, many clients, users, groups,

11
00:00:31.541 --> 00:00:32.846
or all active connections.

12
00:00:32.926 --> 00:00:36.444
That two-way model means hub methods,
message contracts, group routing,

13
00:00:36.524 --> 00:00:40.325
and connection state all deserve the same
careful review given to controllers,

14
00:00:40.405 --> 00:00:42.277
APIs, queues, and background workers.

15
00:00:42.357 --> 00:00:45.932
Real-time applications also introduce
long-lived connection concerns.

16
00:00:46.012 --> 00:00:48.451
Identity may be captured when a connection
starts.

17
00:00:48.531 --> 00:00:51.368
Permissions may change while a connection
remains active.

18
00:00:51.448 --> 00:00:54.455
Reconnect behavior, buffered messages,
streaming, fan-out,

19
00:00:54.535 --> 00:00:58.393
and presence state can create operational
and security questions that ordinary

20
00:00:58.473 --> 00:01:00.516
request and response flows may not expose.

21
00:01:00.596 --> 00:01:02.979
The defensive baseline is explicit
engineering.

22
00:01:03.059 --> 00:01:06.066
Treat every browser, mobile app, desktop
app, service client,

23
00:01:06.146 --> 00:01:09.437
and generated client as a client, not as a
trusted enforcement point.

24
00:01:09.517 --> 00:01:13.035
Put identity, authorization, validation,
resource controls, logging,

25
00:01:13.115 --> 00:01:16.746
and deployment review on the server side
where they can be tested, observed,

26
00:01:16.826 --> 00:01:17.620
and maintained.
